speechtext:
There have been some discussions about the hours of sitting for next week and, if the house is agreeable, perhaps we should make an order somewhat along the following lines:
That in the period ending not later than June 30, 1969, the number of sitting days to be allotted to the consideration of the business of supply shall be reduced to 11;
That in the event of government orders being called on more than one day following the summer adjournment of the house, two sitting days shall be allotted in that part of the current session to the consideration of opposition motions with the proceedings thereon being subject to the provisions of Standing Order 58;
That on Tuesday, June 17, 1969, and Thursday, June 19, 1969, the house shall meet at 10 a.m., rise at 1 p.m., and resume its ordinary hours of daily sitting at 2 p.m., provided that government orders shall be taken up between 10 a.m. and 1 p.m. and that the daity routine of business shall be taken at 2 p.m. in these sittings;
Business of the House
That on Wednesday, June 18, 1969, the hours of sitting shall be 2 p.m. to 6 p.m. and 7 p.m. to 10 p.m.;
That on Friday, June 20, 1969, the hours of sitting shall be 11 a.m. to 1 p.m. and 2 p.m. to 6 p.m.; and
That when proceedings on any government business set down for consideration on any of the said days have been disposed of prior to the hour of adjournment provided for that day, the house shall stand adjourned until the next sitting day, except that on Tuesday and Thursday next the house shall take up the adjournment proceedings.
[DOT] (1:00 p.m.)
If there is general agreement, perhaps it should be made an order, Mr. Speaker.
